  "title": "The Vance oil inquiry raises a question familiar from the Biden Burisma scandal: When does political access become favoritism?",
  "deck": "A senator wants records on whether Vice President JD Vance helped a donor’s company secure a Venezuelan oil license. The Energy Department confirmed a call to the donor, but the public evidence has yet to establish that Vance intervened or that Vitol received preferential treatment.",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "Politics & Energy",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T23:00:00Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-23T23:00:00Z",
  "body": [
    "Two days after U.S. forces captured Nicolás Maduro, Energy Secretary Chris Wright called a Houston oil trader who had helped fund JD Vance’s run for the Senate. The trader, John Addison, told Reuters that Wright asked whether his employer, Vitol, wanted to help sell Venezuelan crude. Within days, Vitol and its rival Trafigura had special licenses to sell the oil.",
    "The Energy Department confirmed that Wright called Addison to discuss Venezuela. Now Sen. Maggie Hassan, a New Hampshire Democrat set to chair the Senate Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs Committee in January, wants to know whether Vance played any role in how Vitol got its license. In a letter reviewed by Reuters, she asked Vance’s office to turn over records by Dec. 14, including communications between Vance or his staff and Addison or Vitol.",
    "### The Montana meeting both men deny",
    "Reuters reported that Vance and Addison discussed Venezuelan oil during a December 2025 trip to Montana, citing a person who said Addison had recounted the conversation. According to that account, Vance asked how quickly the crude could be sold if the United States gained control of Venezuela’s oil supplies. Both men deny the meeting took place. Flight records reviewed by Reuters put their planes at the same Montana airport on Dec. 27. Whether the men spoke remains disputed.",
    "Addison gave $300,000 in 2022 to Protect Ohio Values, a political action committee that backed Vance’s Senate campaign, according to federal campaign finance records reviewed by Reuters. The donation establishes a political tie. What Hassan is asking for is evidence of whether that tie mattered when officials decided who would sell the oil.",
    "### What the Burisma comparison tells us",
    "Hunter Biden held a paid seat on the board of Burisma, a Ukrainian natural gas company, while his father handled U.S. policy toward Ukraine. In a 2019 interview, Hunter acknowledged that his last name probably helped him get the job. Joe Biden later publicly recounted pressing Ukraine to fire prosecutor Viktor Shokin by threatening to withhold a $1 billion U.S. loan guarantee. That pressure was part of Obama administration policy. The public evidence has yet to establish that Joe Biden sought Shokin’s removal to benefit Burisma.",
    "The two cases put the same question to different facts. Burisma paid a vice president’s son to sit on its board. Vitol employed a trader who had helped bankroll a political committee backing Vance. In the Vitol inquiry, the records that matter are the ones that show who recommended the company, why officials chose it and Trafigura, and whether anyone in Vance’s office weighed in. The confirmed call shows how Vitol was approached. How the licensing decision was made is still an open question."
  ],
